WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:17.410
For average person, computer is some sort
of machine which allows them to use certain

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:18.410
applications.

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:23.170
At the same time, mathematically, you can
define a computer as a concrete mathematical

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:25.380
model and study its properties.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:30.660
You can study which computational problems
can be solved fast, which problems cannot

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:31.660
be solved fast.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:35.000
This is done using quite deep mathematical
techniques.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.399
I grew up in this town called Ichalkaranji
in India.

00:00:40.399 --> 00:00:45.030
In both my and my wife's family, everybody's
a doctor, except for me and my wife.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:49.330
And in fact, in the natural course of humans,
I would have ended up being a doctor.

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:51.579
But there was some lucky coincidences.

00:00:51.579 --> 00:00:52.899
I ended up being a mathematician.

00:00:52.899 --> 00:00:58.290
It's a bit difficult for me to say what I
do when I'm not doing math, because that's

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:00.059
what I'm doing most of the time.

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:03.089
But I enjoy a lot playing with my son Neev.

00:01:03.089 --> 00:01:08.810
For me, work and enjoyment or work and relaxing,
these are all really the same.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.600
It might come as a surprise to you that we
are so interested in knowing that certain

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:18.510
computational problems cannot be solved and
in particular cannot be solved fast.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:24.520
We ought to investigate both the power as
well as limitations of our methods.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:30.000
From practical point of view, it's often useful
that certain computational tasks cannot be performed.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.260
For example, I'm sure you would be happy to
know that a hacker cannot break into your

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:38.080
bank account and steal all your money.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.660
One of the important contributions has been
proposing the Unique Games Conjecture and

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.860
then developing research around this conjecture.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:48.740
I was vacationing in India, visiting family.

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:53.800
This small idea came to me that if I used
this Unique Games conjecture as a starting

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:59.380
point of my field of research, it would really
make the whole field easier and very consistent.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:09.840
I will describe the kind of games that I like
to play by giving an example.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:14.510
So this is really a puzzle constructed by
my collaborator Dana Moshkovitz.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:19.090
On the left-hand side you have categories
such as animals, verbs, transportations and

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:20.090
so on.

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:24.700
And on the right-hand side you have a column
of boxes, and you want to fill up these boxes

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:25.980
with letters.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:30.500
So you have to pick out words for different
categories on the left-hand side so they are

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.640
consistent with the letters that you put on
the right-hand side.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.390
So it's a puzzle.

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:39.230
Puzzles of this kind, if they are very large
in size, computers cannot solve these puzzles

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:40.700
fast or efficiently.

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:43.560
So that's really the P not equals NP conjecture.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.640
If the conjecture is true, then would be really
nice.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:51.080
It's as if all the pieces of a big puzzle,
they are kind of fitting together very nicely.

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:57.150
I knew that Assaf Naor nominated me for the
prize this year.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:01.741
It wasn't clear to me what a huge honor this
was until I actually received a call from

00:03:01.741 --> 00:03:04.450
the president of the International Math Union.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:09.350
Receiving the prize does place additional
responsibility on the recipient, but maybe

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:11.819
all these additional responsibilities are
for the better.

